*** Add File:docs/FRONTIER_SELF_HOSTED_README.md
+# Frontier model self-hosting (DeepSpeed/Triton) — README (overview & quickstart)
+
+This document describes the recommended approach to self-host frontier models in Aegis.
+
+Quickstart (PoC)
+1. Package a model artifact using services/frontier/registry/package_model.sh
+2. Upload model artifacts and register metadata:
+   python3 services/frontier/registry/register_model.py --evidence-bucket s3://aegis-evidence-prod --model-name big-llm-v1 --metadata-file ./metadata.json --cosign-key-ref "<kms-ref>"
+3. Deploy the Helm chart:
+   helm -n ml-serving upgrade --install aegis-frontier deploy/frontier/helm/frontier-chart -f deploy/frontier/helm/frontier-chart/values.yaml
+4. Deploy KEDA ScaledObject:
+   kubectl apply -f deploy/frontier/keda/scaledobject.yaml
+5. Run benchmark and tune:
+   kubectl -n ml-serving run bench --image=your-registry/aegis-deepspeed-bench --command -- python /app/bench.py
+
+Notes & tuning
+- Use Triton for low-latency model engines where possible.
+- Use DeepSpeed-Inference for large-sharded transformer models.
+- Configure Prometheus exporters on model servers to emit queue_length and GPU utilization metrics for KEDA.
+
+Security & compliance
+- Ensure model metadata is cosign-signed and stored in S3.
+- Run OPA preflight checks on model promotions.
+- Legal must review model licenses before hosting.
+
